  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts, which builds the whole floating-islands scene procedurally with @aura3d/engine. `typecheck` passes. I could not run `vite build`, and I could not view a rendered frame (no shell or screenshot tool), so how it looks and how fast it runs are untested.\n\n- **Islands:** 12 islands, radii 4 to 18. Each has a grass top and a soil band over a chunky rock underside made of inverted cones and lumps, plus a mound, pine and round trees, and glowing pink or cyan crystals. Each bobs with its own amplitude, speed and phase.\n- **Sky and sun:** An orange-to-pink gradient sky with a low sun. A large sun disc and halo are placed at the sky sun's direction each frame, so they stay aligned as the camera moves.\n- **Clouds:** 14 cloud banks at different depths and heights drift along +x at different speeds and wrap around outside the view.\n- **Waterfall:** A thin tapered water column and 36 falling droplets, with mist at the top and bottom, hang from the rim of the largest island and follow its bobbing.\n- **Haze:** Scene fog in pale lilac-blue makes far islands paler and bluer. I also added light bloom and a vignette.\n- **Camera:** A 50-second closed arc that swings about ±29° with slight height, radius and target sway, driven through `app.camera.setPose` with no user input.\n- **Frame flag:** The engine's loop renders every frame. I set `window.__EVAL_READY__ = true` in a `requestAnimationFrame` callback after the first frame callback.\n\nI used no external assets. A few material settings (`alphaMode: \"blend\"` with `opacity` on the waterfall and halo, `unlit`, `fog: false`) may not behave as I expect. If blending is ignored, the waterfall and halo will look opaque but still show.",
  "finalTypecheckPass": true,
